    "q-106122": {
      "dom_id": "step-q106122",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14224622/assets/30aa4c5e72919d09a0a2323dc8af90a73810047857f3c89fe9c3005a10c195ae.png)\n\nConsider the finite region bounded by the curves $x=4y-{y}^{2}$ and $x=2y,$ as shown above. Find an integral expression for the volume generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis.",
      "worked_solution": "First, let $f(y)=4y-{y}^{2}$ and $g(y)=2y.$\n\nWe start by determining the points where the curves intersect by solving the following equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =g(y) \\\\ 4y-{y}^{2} & =2y \\\\ 2y-{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ y(2-y) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions corresponding to our region are $y=0$ and $y=2.$ So, we need to integrate from $y=0$ to $y=2.$ \n\nNow, let's draw our usual horizontal arrow through the region to determine the left and right functions.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14224622/assets/cbfd6c409590e6b6d350b5ae8f6531944467e90991b17582febe228a6a8dfdb2.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the left function $x=g(y)=2y,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the right function $x=f(y)=4y-{y}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, using the shell method, the volume of the solid can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{c}^{d}y([\\text{right}]-[\\text{left}])\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}y([4y-{y}^{2}]-[2y])\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}y(2y-{y}^{2})\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
