    "q-17023": {
      "dom_id": "step-q17023",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14224622/assets/a5a3a246f2ecb4bee6cfc46f34d14b5abc9fcf22ed0d6173ed465bfab2cfeca6.png)\n\nConsider the finite region bounded by the curves $x=5y-2{y}^{2}$ and $x=y,$ as shown above. Find an integral expression for the volume generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis.",
      "worked_solution": "First, let $f(y)=5y-2{y}^{2}$ and $g(y)=y.$\n\nWe start by determining the points where the curves intersect by solving the following equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =g(y) \\\\ 5y-2{y}^{2} & =y \\\\ 2{y}^{2}-4y & =0 \\\\ 2y(y-2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions are $y=0$ and $y=2.$ So, we need to integrate from $y=0$ to $y=2.$ \n\nNow, let's draw our usual horizontal arrow through the region to determine the left and right functions.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14224622/assets/d873c0acb12392e4ea40f761c72c1903a91c4f0e9fef04b586a71697957e2401.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the left function $x=g(y)=y,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the right function $x=f(y)=5y-2{y}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, using the shell method, the volume of the solid can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{c}^{d}y([\\text{right}]-[\\text{left}])\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}y([5y-2{y}^{2}]-[y])\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}y(4y-2{y}^{2})\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-17023-a",
              "type": "math",
