      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14224622/assets/482781bb21d011df5a9140b2b1fb0ebe1108f746c0055077d4f4e9a91fce735d.png)\n\nConsider the finite region bounded by the curves $y=\\sqrt{2-x},$ $x={y}^{2},$ and the $x$-axis, as shown above. Calculate the volume of the solid generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis.",
      "worked_solution": "First, we write the second function in terms $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\sqrt{2-x} \\\\ {y}^{2} & =2-x \\\\ x & =2-{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let $f(y)={y}^{2}$ and $g(y)=2-{y}^{2}.$\n\nWe start by determining the points where the curves intersect by solving the following equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =g(y) \\\\ {y}^{2} & =2-{y}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ y & =\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution corresponding to our region is $y=1.$ So, we need to integrate from $y=0$ to $y=1.$ \n\nNow, let's draw our usual horizontal arrow through the region to determine the left and right functions.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14224622/assets/48905a174d688fdb3e099debb7840173a2ce047816003a5be9c089997196f9fb.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the left function $x=f(y)={y}^{2},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the right function $x=g(y)=2-{y}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, using the shell method, the volume of the solid can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{c}^{d}y([\\text{right}]-[\\text{left}])\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}y([2-{y}^{2}]-[{y}^{2}])\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}y(2-2{y}^{2})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(y-{y}^{3})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =4\\pi ({\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{3}\\text{d}y) \\\\  & =4\\pi ({\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}|}_{0}^{1}-{\\frac{{y}^{4}}{4}|}_{0}^{1}) \\\\  & =4\\pi ((\\frac{1}{2}-0)-(\\frac{1}{4}-0)) \\\\  & =4\\pi (\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
