    "q-107142": {
      "dom_id": "step-q107142",
      "name": "Question 8",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14224622/assets/920aca572a3b6fc828d6c37246b348aa2b017d1bc4e022ea06c12d0fa09ad6ee.png)\n\nConsider the finite region bounded by the curves $x=(1-y)^{2}$ and $y=\\sqrt{1-x},$ as shown above. Calculate the volume of the solid generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis.",
      "worked_solution": "First, we write the second function in terms $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\sqrt{1-x} \\\\ {y}^{2} & =1-x \\\\ x & =1-{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let $f(y)=1-{y}^{2}$ and $g(y)=(1-y)^{2}.$\n\nWe start by determining the points where the curves intersect by solving the following equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =g(y) \\\\ 1-{y}^{2} & =(1-y)^{2} \\\\ 1-{y}^{2} & =1-2y+{y}^{2} \\\\ 2{y}^{2}-2y & =0 \\\\ 2y(y-1) & =0 \\\\ y & =0,1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to integrate from $y=0$ to $y=1.$ \n\nNow, let's draw our usual horizontal arrow through the region to determine the left and right functions.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14224622/assets/02e6b4204b1edf682dd332e28830dc84aa0e41d3728c66442d777f6fe9daccfc.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the left function $x=g(y)=(1-y)^{2},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the right function $x=f(y)=1-{y}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, using the shell method, the volume of the solid can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{c}^{d}y([\\text{right}]-[\\text{left}])\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}y([1-{y}^{2}]-[(1-y)^{2}])\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}y([1-{y}^{2}]-[1-2y+{y}^{2}])\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}y(2y-2{y}^{2})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(2{y}^{2}-2{y}^{3})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =4\\pi ({\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{3}\\text{d}y) \\\\  & =4\\pi ({\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}|}_{0}^{1}-{\\frac{{y}^{4}}{4}|}_{0}^{1}) \\\\  & =4\\pi ((\\frac{1}{3}-0)-(\\frac{1}{4}-0)) \\\\  & =4\\pi (\\frac{1}{12}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
