    "q-107304": {
      "dom_id": "step-q107304",
      "name": "Question 9",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14224622/assets/5eac1b1d21378d64b90fc2463eb7664b30224594ba5d63aa70a7c412097012a0.png)\n\nConsider the finite region bounded by the curves $x=1+y$ and $x=1-2y+{y}^{2},$ as shown above. Calculate the volume of the solid generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis.",
      "worked_solution": "First, let $f(y)=1+y$ and $g(y)=1-2y+{y}^{2}.$\n\nWe start by determining the points where the curves intersect by solving the following equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =g(y) \\\\ 1+y & =1-2y+{y}^{2} \\\\ 3y-{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ y(3-y) & =0 \\\\ y & =0,3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions corresponding to our region are $y=0$ and $y=3.$ So, we need to integrate from $y=0$ to $y=3.$ \n\nNow, let's draw our usual horizontal arrow through the region to determine the left and right functions.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14224622/assets/84e62b99598649ac48d55171b0d6bf7e5d26e38a6ad94c46ef9c89145e60e29a.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the left function $x=g(y)=1-2y+{y}^{2},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the right function $x=f(y)=1+y.$\n\nTherefore, using the shell method, the volume of the solid can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{c}^{d}y([\\text{right}]-[\\text{left}])\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}y([1+y]-[1-2y+{y}^{2}])\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}y(3y-{y}^{2})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}(3{y}^{2}-{y}^{3})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi (3{\\int }_{0}^{3}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{0}^{3}{y}^{3}\\text{d}y) \\\\  & =2\\pi ({3\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}|}_{0}^{3}-{\\frac{{y}^{4}}{4}|}_{0}^{3}) \\\\  & =2\\pi (3(\\frac{27}{3}-0)-(\\frac{81}{4}-0)) \\\\  & =2\\pi (27-\\frac{81}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{27\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
